    id: 'loan-proceeds-tax-free-income',
    topic: 'debt-credit',
    claim: 'Borrowed money is tax-free income.',
    principle:
      'Loan proceeds are not gross income because the borrower has an offsetting obligation to repay; there is no accession to wealth. That is settled law. But it is not "income" at all, tax-free or otherwise: it is a liability. If the obligation disappears (forgiveness, settlement, foreclosure on recourse debt), the borrower has cancellation-of-debt income under §61(a)(11) unless an exclusion in §108 applies. Debt is a timing and liquidity tool, and the tax system catches up when the debt stops being debt.',
    howItWorks: [
      'Borrowing: no income, no deduction. Repaying principal: no deduction. Interest: deductible or not based on use (see the interest deductibility claim).',
      'Cancellation of debt: when a lender forgives or settles for less, the difference is ordinary income under §61(a)(11), reported on Form 1099-C.',
      'Exclusions under §108: bankruptcy discharge, insolvency (to the extent insolvent), qualified farm and real property business indebtedness, and qualified principal residence indebtedness (extended through 2025); excluded amounts reduce tax attributes (NOLs, basis) under §108(b).',
      'Foreclosure or deed-in-lieu: for recourse debt, treated as a sale for the property\'s fair market value plus COD income for the excess debt forgiven; for non-recourse debt, the full debt is the amount realised (Commissioner v. Tufts, 1983), producing gain rather than COD income.',
      'Death with debt outstanding: the estate repays or the lender collects from the collateral; §1014 step-up applies to the asset, and no COD income arises simply because the borrower died.',
    ],
    whenItWorks: [
      'Borrowing against appreciated assets for liquidity, so long as the debt is serviced or repaid and the borrower understands interest cost and collateral risk.',
      'Debt used for productive purposes where the return exceeds the after-tax cost of interest.',
    ],
    whenItDoesNot: [
      'Treating borrowed money as spendable income without a repayment plan.',
      'Forgiveness or settlement, which generates taxable COD income (often a surprise 1099-C).',
      'Loans from a controlled entity that are really distributions (constructive dividends or compensation).',
      'Shareholder loans from an S corporation or partnership that are never repaid, which also raise basis and distribution issues.',
    ],
    jurisdiction: 'United States federal income tax; most jurisdictions follow the same principle that loan proceeds are not income and that debt release can be taxable.',
    legalMechanism: 'Bona fide debt instruments; lender reporting (Form 1099-C); §108 elections and attribute reduction (Form 982).',
    taxConsequence: 'No income on borrowing; COD income on forgiveness subject to §108 exclusions; gain on disposition of encumbered property; interest deductibility by use.',
    assetProtectionConsequence: 'Debt is a liability a creditor can enforce against collateral and, if recourse, against the borrower\'s other assets. Over-leveraging is itself an asset-protection failure.',
    documentation: ['Loan agreements and notes', 'Payment records', 'Form 1099-C and Form 982 where applicable', 'Insolvency worksheets if claiming the §108(a)(1)(B) exclusion'],
    professionals: ['CPA (COD income, §108, Tufts analysis)', 'Attorney for workouts and settlements'],
    sourceAuthority: ['IRC §61(a)(11) — income from discharge of indebtedness', 'IRC §108 — income from discharge of indebtedness exclusions; §108(b) attribute reduction', 'Commissioner v. Tufts, 461 U.S. 300 (1983)', 'Crane v. Commissioner, 331 U.S. 1 (1947)', 'IRS Publication 4681 — Canceled Debts, Foreclosures, Repossessions and Abandonments'],
